From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Date: Thu, 21 Jul 1994 22:02:55 -0100
Subject: [#94-7-184] Innateness of language [was If Knowledge Limits Learning]

Mr. Price says he believes that a smarter chimp could learn language as
well as a human.

The general concensus in the lingustics community seems to be that human
lingustic aptitude is qualitatively rather than quantitatively different
from simian aptitude.  One important piece of evidence for this is that
human children learn some rules of grammar before they have been exposed to
enough evidence to know that they are the correct rules.  Thus, there must
be some innate bias in the learning process that directs children in the
right direction.

Also, chimp lingustic abilities aren't as good as they look on TV.  They
have essentially no grasp of syntax; much less than a human infant with the
same size vocabulary.

An excellent book on the topic is "the language instinct" by Steven Pinker.
There are a couple of chapters that speak directly to the question of
innateness, and the abilities of chimps to learn language.  The book is
amusing and well written while at the same time being entirely
intellectually solid.

Accuracy rating of this post:  I have an 11-year-old bachelor's degree in
lingustics.  I haven't kept up with the technical literature of the field.
So I think I have a reasonable though not professional grasp of the topic.


