From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Date: Fri, 22 Jul 1994 10:05:22 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-185] Innateness of language [was If Knowledge Limits Learning]

Mr Feynman comments further on innate language structures:
> Also, chimp linguistic abilities aren't as good as they look on TV.
> They have essentially no grasp of syntax; much less than a human
> infant with the same size vocabulary.

There's a useful analogy with autism here.  Suffers from autism exhibit
severe problems in communication and social adjustment.  It is widely
believed that they are lacking the innate concept of other people with
separate thoughts.  Proper training, if caught early enough, can, with
difficulty, correct this problem.  More intelligent suffers make better
adjustments - see "Autism" June Sci Am 1993.  It seems reasonable to
suppose that the successful, more intelligent cases were able to learn
the concept of "other-minds", even though they had no such innate
concept.  For this reason I expect that the concepts of, and facility
with, language syntax would be acquired by sufficiently intelligent
networks, despite having no innate linguistic abilities.

Chimps, BTW, are not autistic, although monkeys do seem to be.  There
was an interesting article on how this was deduced from how they
communicate in "Meaning, Mind and Monkeys" in Dec Sci Am 1992.

