From: Pat <pjgehrke@ecst.csuchico.edu>
Date: Fri, 22 Jul 1994 09:07:32 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-7-187] NanoFlaws?

First time poster, so please be patient.

In my readings on nanotechnology I have been a little disturbed 

by some problems in design that seem fatal to me. 

People like Moravec, Hameroff, Drexler, etc. (and even Feynman I 

suppose) all seem to start from the concept of silicon based 

nanotechnology. I am wondering whether nanotechnology would be 

of equal vulnerability to electromagnetic pulse weapons being 

developed by the Dept. of Defense for "nonlethal" assaults. It 

seems that being complex systems based in silicon with extremely 

low tolerances for error (a micron shift would certainly blow 

any assembler apart) nanosystems would be extremely vulnerable 

to e.m.p. attacks. Lead shielding seems to be a joke, since any 

substantial shielding defeats the concept of it being NANO 

sized. 

A second concern is room temperature. Heat is the vibration of 

molecules. Nanotechnology has very low tolerances. How can a 

system with such low tolerances stay operational when its nano 

sized parts would be vibrating wildly? It seems like tossing a 

calculator in a blender and expecting it to work afterwards. Are

these surmountable problems, and if so, how might they be 

overcome?

-/-Pat Gehrke

