From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 22 Jul 1994 14:21:43 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-190] If Knowledge Limits Learning 

[I am leaving on travel for a week, so my replies will be delayed. RH]

Michael Clive Price writes:
>I believe in "tabula rasa emergent magic AI", ... it may even be
>unlikely, but I consider it far more likely to work than the other,
>symbol processing approaches.

Whoa - there are two independent distinctions: between what Hadon Nash
called "articulated AI" and "tabula-rasa AI", and between symbolic and
neural AI.  The first distinction says how much knowledge you give an AI,
vs.  expecting the AI to discover it.  The second distinction says how
knowledge is represented.  For example, one could take a tabula-rasa
symbolic approach, or an articulated neural approach.

>> If an AI needs only know one part in a thousand of our common sense
>> knowledge to know enough to be trained by humans, this only lowers my
>> time estimate by a factor of two.
>
>You say that training can only begin after the network has reached
>human-level complexity (~5.10^6 chunks). ....  

I meant that to "train" an AI as we train human children, the AIs would
need some substantial fraction (10^-3 above) of adult human common sense
knowledge.  I now see you meant "train" in another sense.

>I say that training starts on an otherwise blank network with "just" a
>low-level learning algorithm.  I envisage a small network with some
>pleasure/pain signal being trained to respond to simple inputs to do
>things like navigate through mazes, sort coloured cubes out and such like
>- simple spatial/motor tasks with no language requirement.  The trainer
>trains by reward and punishment.  A bit like training your dog not to crap
>on the carpet.  Over time the cues increase in complexity and the network
>responds by growing in complexity and intelligence and formulating its own
>sub-goals - this is the "emergent magic" bit - until it starts responding
>to simple commands and, later, full blown English.  I expect that much of
>this early, pre- linguistic training can be automated and run through very
>quickly.  Only the later linguistic input requires human
>tutoring/monitoring (unless symbolic AIs are available!).  This reduces
>the time estimate from 100,000s years to years? months?

Two extreme forms of learning are discovery and being told.  Discovery can
take lots of student time, but requires little teacher effort.  Direct
instruction costs a teacher lots of time, but can make for fast learning
(unless I/0 is a bottleneck, as in humans).  In between are various mixed
approaches, such as "training", where students are asked to discover
within controlled problem contexts.  Such training can allow students to
learn faster than in raw discovery, because the training problems give
students hints about what concepts to use to represent future problems,
and how such problems should be decomposed.  But training also requires
teachers to put lots of thought into choosing problems that encode the
relevant knowledge; not just any small problems will do.

I will certainly grant that a mixed approach may be superior to the two
extremes, but I cannot fathom how it could bring as many orders of
magnitude of improvement as you appear to expect.  It takes an incredible
amount of knowledge to accomplish real world "simple spatial/motor tasks"
- just how many simple training tasks do you think would suffice, to allow
say an AI to run through the woods chasing a rabbit (not much linguistic
knowledge needed there)?

Robin Hanson

